  "strategy": "## Approach and Rationale\n\nThe central idea is to synthesize periodic step functions with low autocorrelation properties to minimize the maximum overlap when shifted. Unlike previous approaches that relied solely on gradient-based optimization with local searches, we use a hybrid strategy combining global random search over predefined reference sequences with local projection methods. We hypothesize that sequences with minimal autocorrelation (like sequences derived from Costas arrays or Golay sequences) naturally satisfy the maximin minimization requirement. This avoids getting trapped in local optima by using complementary starting points drawn from families with proven low-discrepancy properties.\n\nKey theoretical oversight addressed: The problem requires a step function whose autocorrelation is minimal at nonzero shifts. Conventional gradient methods struggle with combinatorial constraints, while standard mutation operators in evolutionary methods often degrade signal structure. Our solution leverages exact constructions from coding theory (which guarantee minimal maximal correlations) and then refines them within a constrained optimization envelope.\n\nLimitations: The approach depends on constructing reference sequences of specific length and cannot be directly extended beyond modest grid sizes. However, the refinements are designed to scale efficiently.\n\n## Mathematical Specification\n\nThe optimization seeks to minimize:\n\\[\n\\max_{k \\in \\mathbb{Z}} \\int_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))\\\\mathrm{d}x\n\\]\nsubject to:\n\\[\n\\int_0^2 h(x)\\\\mathrm{d}x = 1\n\\]\nand:\n\\[\n0 \\le h(x) \\le 1\n\\]\n\nDiscretization on uniform grid $x_i = i \\cdot \\frac{2}{n}\\\\ \\forall i=0,...,n-1$:\n\\[\nh^{\\\\text{grid}} = [h(0), h(1), \\\\dots., h(n-1)]^T \\in \\{0,1\\}^n\n\\]\nwith:\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} h^{\\\\text{grid}}(i) = \\frac{n}{2}\n\\]\n\nLet $c(h^{\\\\text{grid}}, k)$ denote the integrated overlap at shift $k$, then the objective is:\n\\[\nC_5 = \\frac{2}{n} \\cdot \\max_{k=-\\ (n-1)}^{n-1} c(h^{\\\\text{grid}}, k)\n\\]\n\nThe solution strategy combines:\n1. A finite set of reference sequences with low autocorrelation properties (derived using linear algebra)\n2. Local optimization gradient projection updates\n3. Combinatorial mutation operators that preserve integral constraints\n\nThe low-autocorrelation reference sequences $s \\in \\{0,1\\}^n$ are constructed that minimize: \n\\[\n\\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{k=-(n-1)}^{n-1} |\\text{sum}(h \\\\otimes (1-h)[i:i+k]|_2\n\\]\n\nImplementation preserves the binary domain through integral constraint projection:\n\\[\n\\text{Project}(v, t) = \\begin{cases} \\text{Round}(v, t) & \\text{if } \\| \\text{Round}(v,t) - v \\| < \\delta \\epsilon \\\\ v & \\text{if constraint already satisfied} \\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n## Implementation Procedure\n\n1. **Initialization**\n   - **Reference Sequences Setup**: Construct reference sequences of length 512, 1024 using Costas array-derived patterns and Golay complementary sequences\n   - **Population Generation**: Generate candidate pools from:\n     - Provided initial parent sequence (if available)\n     - Costas array-based constructions\n     - Golay complementary sequence-derived candidates\n   - **Validation**: Ensure all candidates have unit integral by projection-layer\n\n2. **Fitness Evaluation**\n   - Compute continuous shift maximin score using FFT-accelerated linear correlation\n   - Use hierarchical early-termination for slow convergence candidates during optimization\n   - Normalize score by factor = \\frac{4}{n-(|k|-1)} for window-dependent overlap\n\n3. **Propagation Strategy**\n   - For each candidate, perform projected gradient descent with:\n     - Momentum parameter $\\beta = 0.9$\n     - Adjusted step size $step_0 = 0.5 \\sqrt{\\text{iter}}$\n     - Noise injection $\\epsilon_{\\text{noise}} = 1e-4$\n   - Maintain cohort diversity through tournament selection\n\n4. **Adaptive Mutation**\n   - **Structural Mutation (0.01 prob)**: Apply random reflection around median signal amplitude\n   - **Integral Enforcement**:\n     - For candidate $h$, compute $n_{\\text{ones}} = \\sum h$\n     - Apply mutation: $h' = h + \\epsilon \\cdot (1 - 2\\text{Bernoulli}(0.5))$\n     - $\\text{Project}(h', n/2)$\n\n5. **Termination Conditions**\n   - Local convergence: 1) No change in top-10 scores for 8 generations OR 2) Normalized score improvement $< 1e-5$\n   - Wall-clock limit: $t_{\\text{refined}} = \\min(\\text{time\\_left}(), 600)$ seconds\n\n6. **Fallback Procedures**\n   - If constrained memory, maintain cohort at size=20 regardless\n   - If projection fails, fall back to iterative Riemann mapping\n   - If complete failure, return best candidate available\n\n## Search Choices and Variations\n\n1. **Cohort Size Selection**: Default=20 with trigger=5 (community competition minimum)\n2. **Wavelet Analysis Trigger**: \n   - Score-based: If running average score < 0.3807, activate spectral decomposition\n   - Time-based: After initial plateau detection at level=90%\n3. **Mutation Scheduling**:\n   - Struct-box mutation: 0.035 probability per candidate\n   - Direct binary mutation: 0.01 probability\n   - Triggered by score degradation >0.5%\n\nGolay-sequence-based variation (5% usage) provides complementary starting points when score stabilizes above 0.3815\n\n## Numerical Robustness & Memory\n\n- Memory Profile: Maintains active cohort of n=20-$\\times $512 floats (\u2248 4.1 MB)\n- uses: `numpy` vectorized operations\n- Fallback projection: `CVXPY`+`ECOS` used only if direct projection fails but time allows\n\nProject implementation first checks for early convergence then accelerates final stages toward exploration boundary",
